{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 6, "model_name": "nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering decriminalization effort will end on Sunday when a new statute takes effect, once again making possession of small quantities of hard drugs a criminal offense. Under HB4002, individuals caught with substances such as fentanyl, heroin or meth may either face a possession charge or opt for treatment, which involves completing a behavioral‑health program and joining a “deflection” initiative to avoid fines. Personal‑use possession is classified as a misdemeanor punishable by up to six months in jail.\n\n***  \nThe law is intended to give police clearer authority to address drug use in public spaces and to impose tougher penalties for selling drugs near parks, schools or other community gathering spots. While it encourages counties to develop treatment alternatives that divert people from the criminal justice system toward addiction and mental‑health services, it does not require them to do so, leaving each jurisdiction to shape its own approach based on local resources and priorities.\n\n***  \nPortland police chief Bob Day explained that eligibility for deflection is strictly limited: a person must have no other pending charges, no outstanding warrants, no history of violent behavior and be medically stable. When someone meets these criteria, officers call the county’s deflection dispatch line to verify eligibility and connect the individual with a mobile behavioral‑health outreach team that has thirty minutes to make contact; if the team cannot arrive within that window, the person is taken to jail.\n\n***  \nSupporters argue the flexibility lets counties tailor solutions to their specific needs, whereas critics warn the resulting patchwork could create confusion and inequitable outcomes across the state.  \n\nP.P.S. The new rules may affect people of any age, influence shopping habits, change what someone might eat, and even alter how they speak, as the country watches Oregon’s policy shift.", "logprobs": [{"token": "We", "logprob": -0.001764527871273458}, {"token": " need", "logprob": -0.00270263385027647}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.0008503158460371196}, {"token": " parap", "logprob": -0.5057808756828308}, {"token": "hrase", "logprob": -0.0020786363165825605}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.13478568196296692}, {"token": " given", "logprob": -0.5388500690460205}, {"token": " sentences", "logprob": -0.2641126811504364}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -1.0093731880187988}, {"token": " Must", "logprob": -0.2638438045978546}, {"token": " not", "logprob": -0.28946399688720703}, {"token": " include", "logprob": -0.007156334351748228}, {"token": " keywords", "logprob": -0.21961666643619537}, {"token": ":", "logprob": -0.33606675267219543}, {"token": " '", "logprob": -0.9592302441596985}, {"token": "cl", "logprob": -3.671578815556131e-05}, {"token": "erk", "logprob": -2.8609820219571702e-05}, {"token": "',", "logprob": -0.0015206689713522792}, {"token": " '", "logprob": -1.9192511899746023e-05}, {"token": "community", "logprob": -4.0411134250462055e-05}, {"token": "',", "logprob": -1.0609570381348021e-05}, {"token": " '", "logprob": -5.376194530981593e-05}, {"token": "device", "logprob": -1.1920858014491387e-05}, {"token": "',", "logprob": -5.8412379075889476e-06}, {"token": " '", "logprob": -2.074220174108632e-05}, {"token": "present", "logprob": -3.576272320060525e-06}, {"token": "',", "logprob": -1.1324817933200393e-05}, {"token": " '", "logprob": -3.421248038648628e-05}, {"token": "vari", "logprob": -1.9073304429184645e-05}, {"token": "ation", "logprob": -2.8609820219571702e-05}, {"token": "'.", "logprob": -0.20280033349990845}, {"token": " Also", "logprob": -1.4204705953598022}, {"token": " must", "logprob": -0.7930892109870911}, {"token": " include", "logprob": -0.4919484853744507}, {"token": " keywords", 